  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday through next Thursday)\nIssued at 1121 PM CDT THU AUG 27 2026\n\nThe early part of the forecast will be controlled by an upper\nlevel shortwave trough that is drifting slowly along the gulf\ncoast. At the surface, north winds will draw in some drier air\ninto the northern part of the forecast area. As a result of these\ntwo features, the best chances of showers and thunderstorms over \nthe 24 to 48 hours will be in the southern part of central\nAlabama, especially the southeast portion. Daily instability\nvalues will be sufficient for thunderstorms, but with little to no\nshear apparent, no organized severe thunderstorm areas are\nexpected at this time -- just your standard summertime afternoon\nstorms.\n\nThe models are in a bit of disagreement in initially handling \nthat upper level low feature. Ultimately, a broad upper level \nridge is forecast to become established early next week, with the \neast-west ridge axis stretching from Arkansas to the Carolinas. \nThe upper low then gets caught up in the tropical easterlies on\nthe south side of the high, and ends up drifting west of the \nMississippi River, becoming less of a factor in our weather. The \nramifications of this are twofold -- keeping daily POPs generally\nbelow climatological normals, and pushing afternoon temperatures \nnoticeably above climatological normals. The Heat Advisories may \nnot yet be done for this summer.\n\n/61/",
